      "{'text': 'This reindeer wearing traditional Sami garb was inspired by my own Sami ancestry.\\nSAMI REINDEER MUSEUM QUALITY GICLEE PRINT is a reproduction of an original gouache painting by Jamie L. Luoto. Printed with archival pigments on archival cotton and alpha cellulose mix, 315gsm, matte finish, fine art paper.', 'timestamp': '2019-04-19 14:23:31', 'url': 'https://www.jamieluoto.com/store/p45/Sami_Reindeer.html'}\n",
      "{'text': 'Last fall I undertook the Ariadne Goddess Pilgrimage to Crete and saw many wonders. Foremost for me was our descent into the Skoteino Cave, following in the footsteps of ancient Cretans who understood the cave to be the Source of Life, the womb of the Goddess, and a place of transformation. I ventured down tentatively taking very wary steps, protecting my two new hip replacements, determined to join our sacred ritual in the cave with my sisters. My hips called a stop to my descent half-way down. I perched perilously on a small rock ledge and there I confronted inner and outer darkness. It was indeed a profound transformation.\\nOne of them is freaking out!\\nI must not scare the bats!\\nWill I die if I fall?\\n‘Of course’ she says ‘we will’.\\ndo I have an inner resource?\\nup and up I do.\\nin this cave for ever.\\nColeen Clare revels in being a great-grandmother to twin girls and sharing in a fourth generation of life. As a teacher and psychologist, she works in the post-adoption community. She was born in Aotearoa and now lives at Gaia’s Garden in Melbourne, Australia. Her connection with our beautiful troubled world comes through womyn’s herstory and the spirit of Goddess. My 2013 Ariadne pilgrimage to Crete with Carol Christ and 15 pilgrims inspired and strengthened me and released a flood of new poetry.\\nA very deep and powerful poem. As a work of art actually filled with light. Thank you for sharing!\\nGorgeous…thank you so much for sharing! I, too, was deeply moved by venturing into Skoteino Cave last year. Blessings!', 'timestamp': '2019-04-20 18:12:40', 'url': 'https://feminismandreligion.com/2013/11/27/inner-and-outer-darkness-in-the-skoteino-cave-by-coleen-clare/'}\n",
      "{'text': 'The first pic is of a speed test I did back on the 19th November; if you look carefully you’ll see the letters TPG; this is the mob that bought out / took over Internode; the ISP I’d signed up with originally.\\nThe download speed was a mighty 1.59; I kid you not, I’m thankful I don’t do much uploading 0.85? Are they kidding?\\nThis second pic is of a test I ran a couple of weeks back when I was able to get onto the ‘net, Note that this is another TPG / Internode test, slight improvement but nothing to write home about!\\nThe last is of a test I did yesterday / today /tomorrow (depends when I finish this load of tosh).\\nNote the new ISP, ‘AussieBroadband’, Note the speeds up and down, or should that be down and up.\\nNote also the ad on the right hand side, please don’t die laughing!\\nGood grief! I’ll never complain again about my ISP … (until the next time). You sure yours is even alive? Looks like some CPR is in order.\\nAnd I thought mine had been running slow!\\nMy landline was dead last week but the Internet was still working albeit a little slow, frequently losing WiFi. Yesterday the landline was fixed from afar and I received a text to say it may take a few days for broadband speeds to increase.\\nIPad result 35.3 download and 7.30 Upload.\\nWell, I guess now I have to stop complaining about my speed!\\nWhen it comes to Internet speeds, Australia doesn’t even rate 3rd world countries status, perhaps it’s because we are so isolated up here, in the Southern Hemisphere!\\nYou’d think the satellites circling the Earth would take care of that.\\nI’m going to check mine right now. I’m not on NBN, however.\\nI haven’t figured out how to do it, despite sending my plea to Ms Google. Fiddle-di-di, I’ll think about it tomorrow. Meanwhile, where is that corkscrew ….\\nHope they manage to reposition some satellites – but maybe it’s a good thing to be isolated?\\nHadn’t thought of those damned overlords, perhaps a sacrifice is in order.', 'timestamp': '2019-04-21 10:45:25', 'url': 'https://lordbeariofbow.com/2019/02/12/i-wasnt-clowning-around-%F0%9F%A4%A1/'}\n",
      "{'text': 'This entry was posted in Plant Nutrition, Urban Gardening, Video Blog and tagged instructions, NPK, nutrient. Bookmark the permalink.\\nNPK liquid prepared in your video based instructions does not tell how much of the liquid in bottle should be used per litreof wter in the hydrophonics tank. The tank could be any size.. Video says add the bottle of prepared solution to the hydroponics tank. Therefore if I have 30 litres tank how much liquid is required to be mixed into the 30 litres tank.?', 'timestamp': '2019-04-25 20:09:03', 'url': 'https://citygreens.in/2018/04/24/video-instructions-preparing-nutrient-solution-using-citygreens-npk-nutrient-solution/'}\n",
      "{'text': 'Advocacy can rarely be done alone. One of the key responsibilities of the advocate is perhaps to obtain a mandate from the very people whose cause she/he is taking forward. Thus building a partnership with the affected is an important step in the process. But this alone is not enough, other important constituents in an advocacy strategy include members of the media, NGOs concerned with the issue, academics, public personalities who can endorse the demand, health groups, legal and so on. The objective of coalition building is not only to engage more constituencies around to issue and build broader consensus and political support around the issue but also to increase resources within the group.\\nIn the process of coalition building the main issue should remain clearly in focus and not get diluted. However together with this process there needs to be consensus built on the specifics- viz. what will be the position of the group on the issue, what are the various outcomes that are desired, who will take what roles and so on. During these discussions the various people involved should discuss which are the non-negotiables, what is the common minimum understanding and what are the areas of compromise.\\nWhich other groups and organisations are working on the issue?\\nAre there any pre-existing networks working on the broad issue?\\nWhat are their stands, approach to the problem? How similar or dissimilar is it?\\nWhat are the relative strengths of these organisations and networks?\\nCan we contribute to their processes and activities, do they need our contributions?\\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of working together with these groups and networks?', 'timestamp': '2019-04-26 16:22:01', 'url': 'https://www.copasah.org/alliance--coalition-building.html'}\n",
